window.SURGERY.sections.push({
  id: "bentall",
  category: "aorta",
  title: "Aortic Root Replacement (Bentall and Valve-Sparing)",
  subtitle: "Replacing the annulus, sinuses and valve as a single unit when the aortic root itself is the diseased segment — composite valve-graft, valve-sparing reimplantation, and their bailouts.",
  readMins: 26,
  day: 9,
  blocks: [
    {
      title: "Start here — the aortic root you forgot",
      figures: [{ id: "bentall-root-anatomy", src: "assets/surgery-generated/bentall/root-anatomy.png", width: 1312, height: 1199, alt: "Cutaway of the native aortic root showing three valve cusps and two coronary ostia in separate sinuses.", caption: "The aortic root extends from the valve annulus through the three sinuses to the sinotubular junction. The right and left coronary arteries originate above the valve from their respective sinuses; the third sinus has no coronary opening." }],
      html: "<p>The aortic root is not the same thing as the ascending aorta, and mixing the two up is the single most common error a trainee makes when this operation is described. The root is the short, specialized segment of the outflow tract that begins where the left ventricular muscle ends and finishes where the tubular ascending aorta begins. It has three parts you must be able to name and picture. The <b>annulus</b> is the fibrous, scalloped ring at the base of the root where the three aortic valve leaflets actually insert — it is not a flat circle but a crown-shaped structure, with each leaflet's attachment rising and falling like a coronet. Above the annulus are the three <b>sinuses of Valsalva</b> — right, left and non-coronary — each a bulge in the aortic wall that sits behind a valve leaflet. Above the sinuses, where the bulges taper back to the calibre of the tubular aorta, is the <b>sinotubular junction (STJ)</b>, a distinct anatomic ridge that is just as important to valve function as the annulus.</p><p>The <b>coronary ostia</b> take off from two of the three sinuses — the right coronary artery from the right sinus, the left main from the left sinus — almost always above the annulus and below or near the STJ; the non-coronary sinus gives rise to no vessel. This matters because any operation on the root has to deal with these two ostia directly: they cannot be left in place if the surrounding aortic wall is being replaced, and they cannot simply be sewn shut. When surgeons say a patient needs <b>root replacement</b> rather than a simple supracoronary (above-the-coronaries) tube graft, they mean the annulus, sinuses and valve are being treated as a single diseased unit and replaced together, with the coronary arteries reimplanted into the new conduit.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Ask yourself, for any aortic aneurysm, one triage question: is the disease confined to the tubular ascending aorta above the sinotubular junction (a supracoronary graft will do), or does it involve the sinuses/annulus themselves (root replacement is required)? Root replacement is a fundamentally different, more complex operation because it obligates coronary reimplantation.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Physiology and pathophysiology — annuloaortic ectasia and functional regurgitation",
      html: "<p>The sinuses of Valsalva are not a passive bulge; they exist because a completely cylindrical aorta right at the valve would let the leaflets suck flush against the aortic wall during systole, occluding the coronary ostia. The sinus geometry creates vortices behind each open leaflet during systole, keeping the leaflet edges away from the wall and keeping the coronary ostia patent for flow, and this same geometry is what allows the leaflets to float closed symmetrically and coapt centrally in diastole. Any process that distorts root geometry threatens both coronary flow and valve competence, which is why root disease and aortic regurgitation are so often the same story.</p><p>The classic root disease is <b>annuloaortic ectasia</b>: symmetric, fusiform dilation of the annulus, sinuses and proximal ascending aorta together, producing the pear- or flask-shaped root seen on imaging. It is the structural signature of connective-tissue disease of the aortic wall. In <b>Marfan syndrome</b>, a mutation in the <b>fibrillin-1</b> gene weakens the microfibrillar scaffold of the aortic media and dysregulates TGF-beta signalling, leading to progressive elastic-fibre fragmentation and cystic medial degeneration. In <b>Loeys-Dietz syndrome</b>, mutations affecting the TGF-beta receptor pathway (TGFBR1/2 and related genes) produce an even more aggressive aortopathy, with dissection occurring at smaller diameters and a tendency toward arterial tortuosity and aneurysms beyond the aortic root. A <b>bicuspid aortic valve</b> carries its own intrinsic aortopathy, independent of the degree of valve stenosis or regurgitation — the aortic wall itself has an abnormal medial architecture, so the root and ascending aorta dilate even in a well-functioning bicuspid valve.</p><p>As the root dilates, both the annulus and the sinotubular junction widen. The valve leaflets themselves do not grow to match, so they are pulled apart and can no longer meet in the centre of the orifice — this produces <b>functional (secondary) aortic regurgitation</b> from an anatomically normal trileaflet valve, distinct from regurgitation caused by primary leaflet disease such as endocarditis or a myxomatous leaflet. And by the law of Laplace, wall tension rises with radius at a given pressure, so as the root enlarges the risk of dissection and rupture accelerates rather than growing in a straight line.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> A dilated root with a structurally normal trileaflet valve and central regurgitation is functional AR from annular/STJ widening — this is exactly the situation a <b>valve-sparing</b> operation is designed to fix, because restoring root geometry alone can restore coaptation without replacing the valve.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Presentation, workup and indications for surgery",
      html: "<p>Root aneurysms are usually silent. Most are found incidentally on imaging obtained for another reason, on a murmur of aortic regurgitation heard at a routine visit, or through family screening once a relative is diagnosed with Marfan or another heritable aortic disease. Symptomatic presentation is a late or catastrophic finding — chest or back pain suggests dissection or impending rupture, and progressive dyspnoea or orthopnoea reflects decompensating severe AR. Because the disease is silent until it isn't, imaging surveillance and threshold-based elective surgery are what actually save these patients' lives; waiting for symptoms means waiting too long.</p><p>Transthoracic echocardiography is the first-line study and is usually how the aneurysm is first found, but definitive sizing for surgical decision-making uses CT angiography or cardiac MRI, measured at end-diastole using consistent, reproducible landmarks (leading-edge-to-leading-edge or inner-to-inner diameter at the sinuses of Valsalva, the widest point of the root) because measurement technique materially changes whether a patient crosses a threshold. Genetic testing and family screening are pursued whenever a heritable connective-tissue disorder is suspected, since the threshold for operating is lower and the rest of the aorta needs lifelong surveillance in these patients.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>General threshold to operate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Degenerative/sporadic root or ascending aneurysm</td><td>&gt;=5.5 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Marfan syndrome</td><td>&gt;=5.0 cm, or &gt;=4.5 cm with risk factors (family history of dissection, growth &gt;=0.3 cm/year, severe AR/MR, planned pregnancy)</td></tr><tr><td>Loeys-Dietz syndrome</td><td>Often 4.0-4.5 cm — lower than Marfan given its more aggressive, smaller-diameter dissection risk; individualized to the specific gene and family history</td></tr><tr><td>Bicuspid aortic valve with root phenotype</td><td>&gt;=5.5 cm, or &gt;=5.0 cm with risk factors (family history of dissection, growth &gt;=0.5 cm/year, coarctation)</td></tr><tr><td>Already undergoing AVR for severe valve disease</td><td>Consider concomitant root replacement at a lower threshold (~4.5 cm)</td></tr><tr><td>Any rapid growth rate</td><td>&gt;=0.5 cm/year (or &gt;=0.3 cm/year in heritable disease) triggers surgery regardless of absolute size</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Outside the elective-size pathway, root replacement is also indicated whenever the root is the site of an <b>acute problem</b>: acute type A aortic dissection with root involvement, and infective endocarditis with a root/annular abscess or destruction that a conservative valve repair or simple replacement cannot fix.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Family history of aortic dissection and documented growth rate are threshold-lowering factors in every category above — a 4.8 cm root that has grown 0.6 cm in a year, or that sits in a family with a dissection death, is treated far more urgently than a static 4.8 cm root found incidentally.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "The operation, step by step — the modified Bentall",
      figures: [{ id: "bentall-composite-graft", src: "assets/surgery-generated/bentall/composite-graft.png", width: 1024, height: 1536, alt: "Composite aortic valve and graft with two coronary button reimplantations and proximal and distal suture lines.", caption: "A modified Bentall seats a prosthetic valve in a tubular graft. The graft is joined to the annulus and distal native aorta, while separate left and right coronary buttons are sewn into its wall, creating four anastomoses to inspect for bleeding." }],
      html: "<p>The modern Bentall operation replaces the annulus, sinuses and valve with a single prefabricated <b>composite valve-graft</b> — a prosthetic valve (mechanical or biologic) sewn into a Dacron tube — and reimplants both coronary arteries into that tube as buttons of native aortic wall. The patient is positioned supine for a median sternotomy, systemically heparinized, and placed on cardiopulmonary bypass (arterial cannulation typically distal ascending aorta or axillary artery if the aorta is diseased near the planned cannulation site; venous cannulation as the concomitant procedure dictates). The aorta is cross-clamped and cardioplegia arrests the heart, exactly as described in the general bypass workflow.</p><p>The aneurysmal ascending aorta is opened and excised down to a few millimetres above the annulus, and the diseased native valve leaflets are excised. The critical, unforgiving step follows: each coronary ostium is mobilized as a <b>button</b> — a circular island of native aortic wall roughly 5 to 8 mm around the ostium — leaving enough surrounding tissue to sew without tension but not so much redundant tissue that the button will kink or fold when reimplanted. The annulus is sized and the appropriately sized composite valve-graft is selected. The prosthetic valve component is sutured into the annulus, typically with pledgeted horizontal mattress sutures placed circumferentially, exactly as in an isolated aortic valve replacement.</p><p>With the valve seated, a buttonhole is cut in the Dacron graft at the position corresponding to each native ostium, and each coronary button is sutured into its buttonhole — first the left, then the right, or vice versa depending on exposure — checking at each step that the button lies without tension, without twisting, and without redundancy that could kink the vessel on reperfusion. The distal end of the graft is then trimmed to length and anastomosed to the native ascending aorta (or into the arch if the disease extends that far, as a hemiarch). The heart is de-aired, the cross-clamp is removed, and the field is inspected for bleeding at all four suture lines — the proximal (valve-to-annulus) line, the two coronary buttons, and the distal anastomosis — before weaning from bypass.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> A Bentall has four suture lines, not one: proximal (valve-to-annulus), left coronary button, right coronary button, and distal (graft-to-aorta). Bleeding after a Bentall is diagnosed by systematically inspecting all four, not by assuming it is always the most obvious line.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Valve-sparing root replacement — David versus Yacoub",
      figures: [{ id: "bentall-david-yacoub", src: "assets/surgery-generated/bentall/david-yacoub.png", width: 1774, height: 887, alt: "Paired cutaways contrasting a native valve enclosed within a graft with a scalloped graft above an unsupported annulus.", caption: "Left: David reimplantation encloses and supports the native annulus within the graft. Right: Yacoub remodeling attaches a scalloped graft to the remaining root tissue while leaving the annular base outside the graft. Both retain the patient's own valve leaflets." }],
      html: "<p>Not every dilated root has a diseased valve. In a young patient with structurally normal, pliable, trileaflet (or a repairable bicuspid) leaflets whose regurgitation is purely functional from annular and STJ widening, replacing a perfectly good native valve with a prosthesis trades one problem (root aneurysm) for a lifetime of prosthesis-related risk (anticoagulation, structural deterioration, endocarditis risk). <b>Valve-sparing root replacement</b> avoids that trade by replacing only the diseased aortic wall around the valve and preserving the native leaflets, restoring the geometry that lets them coapt again.</p><p>The <b>David procedure (reimplantation technique)</b> is the modern default. The native valve is dissected free of the surrounding aortic wall, and a straight (or slightly flared) Dacron tube graft is sutured around the outside of the leaflet complex at the level of the annulus with a row of subannular sutures, effectively resuspending the valve on the inside of the new graft; the sinuses are then recreated or approximated and the coronary buttons reimplanted as in a Bentall. Because the annulus itself is captured and stabilized within the graft, the David procedure directly fixes annular dilation and has proven the more durable option with a lower rate of recurrent AR on long-term follow-up, which is why it has become the preferred valve-sparing technique at most centres.</p><p>The <b>Yacoub procedure (remodeling technique)</b> is the older approach: the graft is scalloped into three tongues that recreate neo-sinuses and are sewn to the top of the excised native sinuses, with the coronary buttons reimplanted into the scallops — but the annulus itself is not reinforced or resuspended. Because the annulus is left free to keep dilating, especially in connective-tissue disease, late recurrent AR and reoperation are more common after remodeling than after reimplantation, and Yacoub has largely fallen out of favour except when combined with an adjunctive external annuloplasty ring to stabilize the annulus.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> David = reimplantation = the valve sits inside the new graft with the annulus captured and stabilized. Yacoub = remodeling = the graft recreates the sinuses on the outside but leaves the annulus unsupported. That single distinction is why David has become the preferred technique.</div><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> Valve-sparing surgery is only appropriate when the leaflets themselves are structurally normal or reliably repairable. Fibrotic, calcified, retracted, prolapsing beyond repair, or endocarditis-destroyed leaflets need a composite valve-graft (Bentall), not a valve-sparing operation.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Bailouts and alternative conduits — Cabrol shunt, homograft and Ross",
      html: "<p>Sometimes a coronary button simply will not reach the graft without dangerous tension — in reoperative surgery, extensive dissection, prior coronary bypass grafts near the ostium, or a heavily scarred, foreshortened native sinus. The bailout is the <b>Cabrol shunt (Cabrol technique)</b>: instead of implanting the button directly, an interposition tube graft (or a small side conduit) connects the coronary ostia to the side of the main aortic graft, relieving tension by giving the coronary blood a longer, unconstrained path to travel. It is a genuinely useful rescue manoeuvre, but it comes at a cost — the interposition graft has a higher long-term rate of occlusion, kinking and pseudoaneurysm formation than a direct button reimplantation, so direct buttons remain the preferred technique whenever they can be achieved without tension.</p><p>When the operation is for <b>infective endocarditis with a root or annular abscess</b>, the choice of conduit changes. Prosthetic material (mechanical valve, Dacron graft) sits in a field that has already proven susceptible to infection, so many surgeons prefer a <b>cryopreserved aortic homograft</b> (allograft) root, which resists reinfection better than prosthetic material, requires no long-term anticoagulation, and can be tailored to reconstruct destroyed annular tissue. Its drawbacks are limited availability, structural degeneration over 10 to 15 years, and a technically demanding reoperation if it eventually fails.</p><p>The <b>Ross procedure (pulmonary autograft)</b> takes the concept further: the patient's own pulmonary valve and root are excised and moved into the aortic position (the autograft), and the pulmonary position is then reconstructed with a homograft. Because the autograft is living, autologous tissue, it can grow (relevant in children) and needs no anticoagulation, which makes it attractive for young patients who want to avoid lifelong warfarin. The trade is complexity and dual risk: the operation becomes, in effect, two valve operations at once, with late failure possible at either the aortic autograft (which can dilate) or the pulmonary homograft (which degenerates), and it is best reserved for specialized centres with a track record in the procedure.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Match the conduit to the problem: direct coronary buttons whenever feasible; a Cabrol shunt when a button will not reach without tension; a homograft when active infection makes you distrust prosthetic material; a Ross when a young patient's growth potential and desire to avoid anticoagulation outweigh the added complexity of a two-valve operation.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Complications and how to manage them",
      html: "<p>Most Bentall-specific complications trace directly back to the coronary buttons, because ostial tissue is thin, friable and unforgiving of tension or malposition. <b>Button bleeding</b> at the fine anastomotic suture line is common in the first hours after reperfusion and usually responds to local pressure, additional pledgeted sutures, and correction of coagulopathy; persistent bleeding despite these measures needs re-exploration. <b>Kinking or twisting</b> of a button — from excess redundant length, an unrecognised rotational mismatch between the button and the graft, or a button implanted at the wrong height — causes acute regional ischaemia and can present as ST-changes, arrhythmia or a low-output state immediately on weaning from bypass; the fix is prompt recognition on TEE/ECG and surgical revision of the button before the myocardium is irreversibly injured. <b>Tension</b> on an inadequately mobilized button risks either ischaemia (the vessel is stretched and narrowed) or frank avulsion of the button from the graft, a surgical emergency requiring immediate repair or conversion to a Cabrol shunt. Late, a weak point at the button-to-graft suture line can form a <b>coronary button pseudoaneurysm</b>, often found incidentally on surveillance CT years later and treated with reintervention (open repair, covered stent, or reoperation) once identified, because it carries a rupture risk.</p><p>Beyond the buttons, generalized <b>hemorrhage</b> from the multiple suture lines after a long bypass and cross-clamp time reflects both surgical bleeding and dilutional/consumptive coagulopathy; management follows the same principles as any post-bypass bleeding — protamine reversal, correction of coagulation factors and platelets, warming, and re-exploration for a surgical source that does not respond. <b>Valve or conduit thrombosis</b> is a mechanical-valve-specific risk, more likely with sub-therapeutic anticoagulation or a low-flow state, presenting with new murmur change, heart failure or embolic events, and confirmed with echocardiography (fluoroscopy for leaflet motion); management ranges from thrombolysis to emergency reoperation depending on severity. Tamponade from a contained mediastinal collection and low cardiac output from myocardial stunning after a long cross-clamp time are managed with the same general post-cardiotomy principles as any open-heart case.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> New ST-segment change, ventricular arrhythmia, or a low-output state immediately after weaning from bypass following a root replacement should make you think first of a kinked, twisted or tensioned coronary button before reaching for inotropes alone — this is a mechanical surgical problem until proven otherwise.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Anticoagulation and long-term implications of the conduit choice",
      html: "<p>The choice between a mechanical and a biologic (bioprosthetic, tissue) valve within the composite conduit carries the same trade-offs as an isolated aortic valve replacement, but the consequences are magnified because the entire root and coronary reimplantation are on the line if the valve later needs replacing. A <b>mechanical composite valve-graft</b> requires <b>lifelong warfarin</b> anticoagulation — typically targeting an INR in the range of 2.5 to 3.5 for an aortic mechanical valve, often with low-dose aspirin added — because mechanical leaflets are thrombogenic surfaces and thrombosis or embolism is a lifelong risk without adequate anticoagulation. In exchange, a well-functioning mechanical valve essentially does not structurally wear out, making it attractive for younger patients who can safely manage anticoagulation and who want to avoid a future reoperation for valve degeneration.</p><p>A <b>biologic (bioprosthetic) composite valve-graft</b> avoids the need for lifelong warfarin — patients typically need only a brief period of anticoagulation or antiplatelet therapy after surgery — but the tissue valve undergoes gradual structural deterioration, with a meaningful risk of needing reoperation or transcatheter valve-in-valve intervention within one to two decades, faster in younger patients whose calcium metabolism accelerates tissue degeneration. The choice is therefore individualized around age, bleeding risk, lifestyle, anticoagulation compliance, and patient preference — the same conversation as any AVR, simply embedded inside a bigger operation.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Homografts and the Ross autograft need no long-term anticoagulation at all — that is precisely why they are attractive in young patients and in active endocarditis, at the cost of either limited durability (homograft) or added surgical complexity (Ross).</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named techniques and connective-tissue disease",
      html: "<p>At the ceiling you should be able to rattle off the size thresholds cold: degenerative aneurysm at 5.5 cm; Marfan syndrome at 5.0 cm (4.5 cm with risk factors such as family history of dissection, growth &gt;=0.3 cm/year, or planned pregnancy); Loeys-Dietz syndrome operated at smaller diameters still (often 4.0 to 4.5 cm) because it dissects at smaller sizes than Marfan; bicuspid aortic valve with a root phenotype at 5.5 cm (5.0 cm with risk factors); and a lower threshold (~4.5 cm) whenever the patient is already having the aortic valve replaced for other reasons. Growth rate — generally &gt;=0.5 cm/year, or &gt;=0.3 cm/year in heritable disease — is an independent trigger regardless of the absolute diameter reached.</p><p>Know the eponyms and what distinguishes them: <b>Bentall</b> (composite valve-graft with direct coronary button reimplantation) versus <b>David</b> (valve-sparing reimplantation, annulus captured and stabilized inside the graft — the modern preferred valve-sparing technique) versus <b>Yacoub</b> (valve-sparing remodeling, neo-sinuses recreated but the annulus left unsupported, more prone to late recurrent AR) versus <b>Cabrol</b> (an interposition graft bailout for a coronary button that will not reach without tension, carrying a higher late occlusion/pseudoaneurysm rate than a direct button) versus the <b>Ross</b> (pulmonary autograft to the aortic position with a homograft replacing the pulmonary root, needing no anticoagulation but converting one valve problem into a two-valve operation).</p><p>Know why endocarditis with root/annular abscess pushes toward a <b>homograft</b> rather than prosthetic material — resistance to reinfection — and why connective-tissue disease patients need lifelong downstream aortic surveillance after root replacement, since the rest of their aorta (arch, descending, abdominal) remains at elevated dissection risk from the same underlying wall defect even after the root itself has been fixed. And know the anticoagulation targets cold: INR 2.5-3.5 for a mechanical aortic composite valve, versus no lifelong anticoagulation for a bioprosthetic conduit, homograft, or Ross autograft.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> Root replacement techniques by what happens to the annulus and valve: <b>Bentall</b> = valve replaced, in a graft, buttons sewn direct. <b>David</b> = valve spared, annulus captured inside the graft. <b>Yacoub</b> = valve spared, annulus left free. <b>Cabrol</b> = a bailout conduit, not a root-replacement technique in itself. <b>Ross</b> = valve replaced by the patient's own pulmonary valve, pulmonary valve replaced by a homograft.</div>"
    }
  ],
  steps: [
    "Position supine, perform median sternotomy, open the pericardium, and expose the aortic root and ascending aorta",
    "Systemically heparinize, cannulate the ascending aorta or a safe alternative inflow site and the right atrium, then institute cardiopulmonary bypass",
    "Cross-clamp the aorta and deliver cardioplegia to arrest the heart",
    "Open the aneurysmal ascending aorta and inspect the root, valve leaflets and coronary ostia",
    "Excise the diseased aortic wall down to a few millimetres above the annulus and excise the native valve leaflets",
    "Mobilize the left and right coronary ostia as buttons of native aortic wall, leaving enough tissue to reach without tension",
    "Size the annulus and select the appropriately sized composite valve-graft conduit",
    "Suture the prosthetic valve component into the annulus with pledgeted horizontal mattress sutures",
    "Cut a buttonhole in the graft and reimplant the left coronary button, checking for tension-free, non-kinked lie",
    "Reimplant the right coronary button in the same fashion, confirming correct rotational orientation",
    "Inspect both button anastomoses for tension, twist, or kinking; use a Cabrol interposition graft only if direct reimplantation cannot lie safely",
    "Trim the distal graft to length and complete the distal anastomosis to the native ascending aorta or arch",
    "De-air the heart and aortic root thoroughly before releasing the cross-clamp",
    "Release the cross-clamp and reperfuse, inspecting all four suture lines under increasing pressure for hemostasis",
    "Wean from cardiopulmonary bypass, confirming valve function and coronary flow with TEE",
    "Give protamine and secure hemostasis at the proximal, both coronary button, and distal suture lines",
    "Place epicardial pacing wires and mediastinal drains",
    "Close the sternotomy in standard fashion"
  ]
});

